  "query_preview": "..\nVisible satellite imagery clear skies over the western half of\nArizona with cloudy conditions in the far southeastern corner of\nthe state. The trough that brought these cloudy skies exiting\nthe state and entering New Mexico. Eastern locations are\nexperiencing westerly breezes and it will continue through the\nremainder of the day and diminishing in the evening. Cold air\nwill sink into the region, bringing a colder night for Southeast\nArizona even though there will be high cloud cover moving across\nthe region...potentially limiting cold temperatures. Nether the\nless, Sulphur Springs Valley, Upper San Pedro River Valley, and\nsouthern Greenlee County can experience their first freeze of \nthe season. Therefore, a Freeze Warning is in effect for those \nlocations from 1 AM MST through 8 AM MST Sunday. \n\nEnsemble models show a progressive weather pattern through the\nnew week. First, a minor shortwave ridge will move over the\nregion tomorrow, bringing fairly benign conditions and a warming\ntrend. By Monday, the ensemble models have consensus on a\nshortwave passing through the northern half of Arizona, bringing cooler\ntemperatures, especially on Tuesday morning. Sulphur Springs, \nUpper San Pedro Valley, and southern Greenlee County has 25-50% \nchances for min temperatures of 32F degrees or colder. Portions\nof Santa Cruz and southeast Pima County has 5-20% chances for a\nfreeze, a scenario to look for as we get closer to Monday and \nhow much of the cold air will stay after the trough passes. \n\nThere is a quite a discrepancy on the next potential weather \nsystem within the ensemble model world. One general consensus \nfrom the ensembles is a ridge of high pressure building and \nmoving into the region from the south Pacific with the \nforecasted ridge axis laying over southern California to the \nDakotas. As it builds, a minor shortwave trough will ride over \nit...along with the aid of another closed low in the north \nPacific, kicking off the atmospheric ripple...and clipping \nArizona by Wednesday to Thursday. What the ensembles disagree on\nis the evolution of this shortwave trough. 16% of combined \nensemble members in the cluster analysis (0% ENS, 0% GEPS, and \n53% GEFS) has a scenario where the trough will kick out a closed\nupper low and move over southeast Arizona. A stark contrast \nfrom the GEPS and ENS members...and even the AIFS ENS members. \nGEFs scenario would bring cooler temperatures and low chances \nfor precipitation; even through the closed low is not in an \nideal position to draw up moisture for precipitation. Yet, the \nleading scenario (50% of all combined ensemble members in \ncluster analysis: 72% ENS, 60% GEPS, and 7% GEFS) has a \nshortwave just brushing by the region, keeping temperatures near\nnormal. Therefore, this forecast package has generally benign \nconditions to reflect the leading scenario. This will be worth \nwatching to see how the ensemble models initialize and perform\nwith the Pacific northwest low, especially the GEFS dispersion \nchallenge, in the coming runs. \n\nAfter Thanksgiving, the ridge of high pressure will move over \nthe region either behind the closed low scenario or the minor \nshortwave trough scenarios. (Ensembles are in agreement on the\nridge building and moving.) Temperatures will be trending \nwarmer and rising to slightly above normal by the start of the \nnext weekend. The upper level pattern will still be progressive \nwith another weather system, potentially bringing chances for \nprecipitation for the following week.",
